of the car as C.\n\nFrom the first condition:\n\nAt 10 minutes, the car catches up to the truck. The car\'s speed relative to the truck is C - T. The time taken for the car to catch up is 10 minutes. The initial distance between the car and truck is b - 0 = b (since truck is at 0). Wait, but in our new notation, at t=0, the truck is at 0, car is at c, bus is at b.\n\nWait, this inconsistency needs to be resolved. Let\'s clarify.\n\nIn previous notation with truck at 0, bus at position b, car at position c. The truck is at 0, car is at c, bus at b. The car catches the truck at t=10 min. So, if car is behind the truck (since c is the car\'s position and it needs to catch up to truck at 0), then in this scenario, the car is starting at position c (behind the truck) and catches up at 10 min. So, relative speed is C - T (speed of car with respect to truck). Distance to cover is from position c to 0. So:\n\nTime taken = 10 minutes = distance / relative speed = |c| / (C - T)\n\nBut since car is behind the truck, C > C_car? Wait, no:\n\nWait, car\'s speed is C (relative to ground). Truck\'s speed is T (relative to ground). Relative speed of car with respect to truck is C - T (if car is faster, then moving at C - T relative to truck).\n\nBut when the car is starting behind the truck, catching up would mean that C > T. The time taken to cover distance |c| is |c| / (C - T) =10 minutes.\n\nSimilarly, at t=15 minutes, car catches up to the bus. The bus is ahead at position b. Since the car is starting at 0 (truck position) at t=10 min and then continues moving, so by t=15 min (another 5 minutes), the car has been moving at speed C, so it has traveled 5C. In that time, the bus has been moving at speed B, so from t=10 min to 15 min, it moves 5B. The position of the bus at 15 min is b +5B. The car\'s position at 15 min is 10(C - T) + C (since from t=0 to 10 min, it was moving at speed C, covering 10C, but that\'s not correct. Wait, if car catches up to truck at 10 minutes, having traveled 10 minutes at speed C relative to truck, but not sure how to model the car\'s total distance after that.\n\nWait, perhaps better to use relative positions.\n\nAt t=10 min, car is at truck\'s position (0). Then, from t=10 min to 15 min (another 5 min), car moves at speed C, so adds 5C. So, car\'s position at 15 min is 5C.\n\nBus\'s position from t=0 to 15 min: starts at b, moves at speed B, so at 15 min: b +15B.\n\nHowever, car and bus meet at 15 min, so 5C = b +15B.\n\nSimilarly, from the earlier condition, at t=10 min, we had c +10v_C = 10v_T, but in this notation:\n\nIf at t=0, the car is at position c, speed of car is C, then in 10 minutes, car\'s position is c +10C. Truck\'s position is 0 +10T. They meet, so:\n\nc +10C =10T → c =10(T - C)\n\nSimilarly, at t=15 min, bus is at b +15B, car is at 5C, and c =10(T - C) = car\'s position. But wait, now the car\'s position at t=10 min is c +10C (from above) but at t=10 min, the car has already moved, so maybe this notation is conflicting.\n\nWait, maybe my previous notation is getting messy. Let me try again.\n\nLet me redefine variables:\n\nLet me consider that at time t=0:\n\n- The truck is at position 0.\n\n- The bus is at position x (since it\'s ahead, so positive x).\n\n- The car is at position -y (since it\'s behind, so negative x).\n\nGiven that initially, the truck is exactly in the middle between the bus and the car. Therefore, the average of the positions of the bus and the car is the position of the truck:\n\n( x + (-y) ) /2 =0 (since the truck is at 0), so (x - y)/2=0 → x = y.\n\nSo, x = y.\n\nSo, at t=0:\n\nBus at x, car at -x, truck at 0.\n\nFrom this initial state:\n\nAfter 10 minutes:\n\nTruck moves to 10T (speed of truck).\n\nCar moves to -x +10C (speed of car).\n\nThey meet, so their positions are equal:\n\n- x +10C =10T.\n\nThus,\n\n-x +10C =10T → -x =10(T - C )\n\nAfter another 5 minutes (total 15 minutes), the car catches up to the bus.\n\nBus is now at x +15B (speed of bus).\n\nCar is at -x +10C +5C = -x +5C (since car moves at speed C after truck is caught at 10 minutes).\n\nWait, this is where confusion arises. If the car catches the truck at 10 minutes, then from 10 to 15 minutes, the car is moving at speed C, so it would cover an additional 5C distance. However, during the same time, the bus is moving at speed B. So, the position of the bus at 15 minutes is x +15B, and the car\'s position at 15 minutes is the position it reached when catching the truck plus 5C. But need to clarify.\n\nWait, perhaps better:\n\nFrom t=0 to t=10 minutes:\n\n- Truck moves to 10T\n\n- Bus moves to x +10B\n\n- Car moves to -x +10C\n\nAt t=10 minutes, car catches up to truck: positions equal.\n\nSo,\n\n-x +10C =10T\n\nWhich gives:\n\n-x =10(T - C )\n\nSimilarly, from 10 to15 minutes:\n\n- Bus moves to x +10B +5B =x +15B\n\n- Truck moves to 10T +5T =10T +5T=15T (if it keeps moving at speed T)\n\nWait, but if in previous steps the truck was moving at speed T, then from t=0 to10 minutes, it moves 10T, so speed T. Then from 10-15 minutes, another 5T. So, at t=15 minutes, truck is at 10T +5T=15T.\n\nBut the problem states that at t=15 minutes, the car catches up to the bus. So their positions are equal:\n\nPosition of car: position at t=10 minutes +10 minutes speed of car.\n\nWait, no: after catching the truck at t=10 minutes, the car\'s speed changes? The problem doesn\'t mention acceleration or deceleration, it states that each maintains constant speed. So, after t=10 minutes, the car continues moving at its constant speed C. Similarly, the bus is moving at speed B.\n\nThus, at t=15 minutes:\n\n- Position of bus: initial position at t=10 minutes is x +10B, then moving another 5B at t=15 minutes: x +10B +5B =x +15B\n\n- Position of car: at t=10 minutes, position is -x +10C, then moving at speed C for next 5 minutes: -x +10C +5C =-x +15C\n\nThe car catches up to the bus at t=15 minutes, so their positions equal:\n\n-x +15C =x +15B\n\nBring x to the right and solve:\n\n-15C = 